---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import explorer from "../../data/massppe/explorer.json";
import western from "../../data/massppe/districts/western.json";
import { change, digits } from "../../lib/massppe/format";
import { cagr, linearFit } from "../../lib/massppe/stats";
import { BLUE } from "../../lib/massppe/palette";
// How the cards under each chart get their two figures, the trend line's change and the actual change, worked through
// with Leverett's in-district pupils (Tim, #265, after #264: "my brain has trouble accepting that it can be as big a
// delta as −2.6% and −19.5%"). Each drawer of cards links here (explorer.ts, drawTotals). The numbers are worked at
// build time from the explorer's own data with the explorer's own fit (stats.ts), so they match the card's.
const years = explorer.meta.years as number[];
const lev = (western.entities as { id: string; name: string; enroll: { in_district: (number | null)[] }; students: { total: (number | null)[] } }[]).find((e) => e.id === "leverett")!;
const pupils = lev.enroll.in_district.map((v) => v!);
// DESE's separate October 1 head count, which shows the same jump
const heads = lev.students.total;
const n = pupils.length;
const last = n - 1;
const [fy0, fy1] = [years[0], years[last]];
const meanX = (n - 1) / 2;
const meanY = pupils.reduce((a, v) => a + v, 0) / n;
const sxy = pupils.reduce((a, v, i) => a + (i - meanX) * (v - meanY), 0);
const sxx = pupils.reduce((a, _, i) => a + (i - meanX) ** 2, 0);
const slope = sxy / sxx;
const trend = linearFit(pupils).map((v) => v!);
const actual = pupils[last] / pupils[0] - 1;
const along = trend[last] / trend[0] - 1;
const rate = cagr(trend[0], trend[last], last)!;
const gap = pupils.map((v, i) => v - trend[i]);
// The page's account is of these years' shape: a first year below the line, a last year above it, and two changes far
// apart. New data that changes that should stop the build, not publish a page whose words no longer fit its numbers.
if (!(gap[0] < -10 && gap[last] > 0 && actual - along > 0.1)) throw new Error("trend-and-change: Leverett's enrollment no longer fits this page's account");
const avg = (from: number, to: number) => {
  const part = pupils.filter((_, i) => years[i] >= from && years[i] <= to);
  return part.reduce((a, v) => a + v, 0) / part.length;
};
const [high, since] = [avg(2006, 2011), avg(2014, fy1)];

const one = (v: number) => digits(v, 1);
const signedOne = (v: number) => `${v < 0 ? "−" : "+"}${digits(Math.abs(v), 1)}`;

// The chart: the explorer's own marks, drawn here as a fixed picture
const [W, H, L, R, T, B] = [760, 300, 84, 64, 14, 34];
const yMax = 200;
const x = (i: number) => L + (i * (W - L - R)) / last;
const y = (v: number) => T + (1 - v / yMax) * (H - T - B);
const path = (vs: number[]) => vs.map((v, i) => `${i ? "L" : "M"}${x(i).toFixed(1)},${y(v).toFixed(1)}`).join("");
const ticks = [0, 50, 100, 150, 200];
const TREND = "#b65c3d";
---

<Layout title="Trend and actual change, worked through - Tim Shores"
  description="How the Municipal spending explorer calculates a chart's trend line and its actual change, worked through with Leverett's enrollment">
  <p class="back"><a href="/muni-spending/?d=leverett">Municipal spending explorer</a></p>
  <h1>Trend and actual change, worked through</h1>

  <p>
    Under each chart, the explorer's cards give two figures for the same years: the change along the trend line, and
    the actual change from the first year to the last. Sometimes they are far apart. {lev.name}'s in-district pupils
    are an example. From fiscal year {fy0} to fiscal year {fy1} (FY{fy0} to FY{fy1}), the actual change is
    {change(actual)}, and the change along the trend line is {change(along)}. Both figures are correct.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${W} ${H}`} role="img"
      aria-label={`${lev.name}'s in-district pupils, FY${fy0} to FY${fy1}, with the trend line. Pupils go from ${one(pupils[0])} to ${one(pupils[last])}. The trend line goes from ${one(trend[0])} to ${one(trend[last])}.`}>
      {ticks.map((t) => (
        <g>
          <line x1={36} x2={W - R} y1={y(t)} y2={y(t)} class="grid" />
          <text x={28} y={y(t)} dy="0.32em" text-anchor="end" class="tick">{t}</text>
        </g>
      ))}
      {years.map((yr, i) => (i % 5 === 0 ? <text x={x(i)} y={H - B + 18} text-anchor="middle" class="tick">{yr}</text> : null))}
      <path d={path(trend)} class="trend" stroke={TREND} />
      <path d={path(pupils)} class="line" stroke={BLUE} />
      {pupils.map((v, i) => <circle cx={x(i)} cy={y(v)} r={i === 0 || i === last ? 4.5 : 3} fill={BLUE} class="dot" />)}
      <text x={x(0) - 9} y={y(pupils[0])} dy="0.32em" text-anchor="end" class="end" fill={BLUE}>{one(pupils[0])}</text>
      <text x={x(last) + 9} y={y(pupils[last])} dy="-0.1em" class="end" fill={BLUE}>{one(pupils[last])}</text>
      <text x={x(0) - 9} y={y(trend[0])} dy="0.32em" text-anchor="end" class="end" fill={TREND}>{one(trend[0])}</text>
      <text x={x(last) + 9} y={y(trend[last])} dy="0.9em" class="end" fill={TREND}>{one(trend[last])}</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      <span class="key" style={`--c:${BLUE}`}></span> {lev.name}'s in-district pupils each year
      <span class="key dash" style={`--c:${TREND}`}></span> Trend line
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The actual change uses two years</h2>
  <p>
    {lev.name}'s schools taught {one(pupils[0])} pupils in FY{fy0} and {one(pupils[last])} in FY{fy1}. (These are
    full-time equivalents, so a pupil enrolled for half the year counts as half.) The actual change divides the last
    year by the first:
  </p>
  <p class="work">{one(pupils[last])} ÷ {one(pupils[0])} − 1 = {change(actual)}</p>
  <p>The {n - 2} years between them play no part in it.</p>

  <h2>The trend line uses every year</h2>
  <p>
    The trend line is the straight line that comes closest to all {n} years at once. "Closest" means the line with the
    smallest sum of squared distances from each year's figure, called a least-squares line.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Number the years from 0 (FY{fy0}) to {last} (FY{fy1}). The middle year is {digits(meanX)}, which is FY{years[Math.round(meanX)]}.</li>
    <li>Average the pupils over all {n} years: {one(meanY)}. The line passes through that average at the middle year.</li>
    <li>
      Find the slope. For each year, multiply its distance from the middle year by its pupils' distance from the
      average, and add those up: {signedOne(sxy)}. Divide by the sum of the squared distances from the middle year,
      {digits(sxx)}. The slope is {signedOne(slope)} pupils a year (−{digits(Math.abs(slope), 2)} before rounding).
    </li>
    <li>
      Follow the line out to both ends. {digits(meanX)} years before the middle it stands at {one(trend[0])}, and
      {digits(meanX)} years after at {one(trend[last])}.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>The change along the trend line divides the line's last year by its first, as the actual change does with the pupils:</p>
  <p class="work">{one(trend[last])} ÷ {one(trend[0])} − 1 = {change(along)}</p>
  <p>
    The card's yearly figures come from the same line. It falls {one(Math.abs(slope))} pupils each year. As a yearly
    rate that compounds from {one(trend[0])} to {one(trend[last])} over {last} years, that's {change(rate)} a year.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the two differ here</h2>
  <p>
    The actual change starts from FY{fy0}'s {one(pupils[0])} pupils. The trend line starts from {one(trend[0])},
    which is {one(Math.abs(gap[0]))} more, because the years after FY{fy0} pull the line up. Enrollment rose to
    {one(pupils[1])} in FY{years[1]} and averaged {one(high)} from FY2006 to FY2011. It fell back by FY2014 and has
    averaged {one(since)} since then. At the other end, FY{fy1}'s {one(pupils[last])} pupils are {one(gap[last])} above
    the line.
  </p>
  <p>
    A change measured between those two years is small. A line fitted to high years early and lower years late slopes
    down.
  </p>
  <p>
    The jump in FY{years[1]} is in the state's figures, not a slip in the explorer. The Department of Elementary and
    Secondary Education's separate head count on October 1 shows it too: {digits(heads[0]!)} students in the FY{fy0} school year
    and {digits(heads[1]!)} the next.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Fiscal year</th><th class="num">Pupils</th><th class="num">Trend line</th><th class="num">Pupils minus trend line</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {years.map((yr, i) => (
        <tr class={i === 0 || i === last ? "end-row" : undefined}>
          <th scope="row">FY{yr}</th>
          <td class="num">{one(pupils[i])}</td>
          <td class="num">{one(trend[i])}</td>
          <td class="num">{signedOne(gap[i])}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Reading the two figures together</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The actual change is exact, and anyone can check it against the chart. It depends on two years only, so an
      unusual first or last year moves it a lot.
    </li>
    <li>
      The trend line uses every year, so one unusual year moves it less than it moves the actual change. It assumes
      the change was steady. Where
      the years rose and then fell, as {lev.name}'s pupils did, a straight line describes them poorly.
    </li>
    <li>
      When the two figures are far apart, the first year or the last sits far from the trend line, and the change
      between them was not steady. Look at the chart's line to see what happened in between.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The explorer fits each trend line to the years the chart shows, so switching on Since 1990 gives a different line.
    On a chart in dollars, the line is fitted to the dollars the chart shows, before or after inflation as the Dollars
    switch is set.
  </p>

  <h2>Trend breaks</h2>
  <p>
    One straight line can't follow years that rose and then fell. The Trend breaks choice, under each chart and in the
    display options, lets the trend line turn at FY2015, or at FY2010, FY2015 and FY2020. The line stays connected:
    it is straight between two breaks, it can change direction at a break, and it never jumps. The cards then give
    each stretch's yearly figure and its own actual change. The breaks are the same years on every chart. A stretch
    with fewer than three years of figures joins the one beside it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The connected line is still one least-squares calculation over every year the chart shows. With no breaks the
    calculation settles two numbers, the line's starting height and its slope. Each break adds one more, the amount
    the slope changes at that year, and all of them are chosen together to make the sum of squared gaps as small as it
    can be. This is called a linear spline, or segmented regression.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fitting each stretch separately would leave two heights at every break year, one where the earlier line stops and
    one where the later line starts. The break years are calendar years, not events, so nothing suggests a jump at
    them, and a connected line treats the stretches as parts of one longer trend. Because the stretches share one
    calculation, a stretch's slope depends a little on the years beside it. Setting the chart's years to FY2015
    through FY2020 fits a line to those six years alone, and its slope will differ from the FY2015–20 stretch of a
    line fitted to FY2005 through FY2025.
  </p>
  <p>
    Broken every five years, {lev.name}'s in-district pupils show what the single line hides. Enrollment rose from
    FY2005 to FY2010, fell from FY2010 to FY2015, and has changed little since.
  </p>
</Layout>

<style>
  .back {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }

  .back a::before {
    content: "← ";
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin: 1.6rem 0 0.4rem;
  }

  figure {
    margin: 1rem 0 1.2rem;
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .grid {
    stroke: var(--line);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }

  .tick {
    font-size: 11px;
    fill: var(--ink-soft);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .line,
  .trend {
    fill: none;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }

  .line {
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  .trend {
    stroke-width: 2.25;
    stroke-dasharray: 7 5;
  }

  .dot {
    stroke: var(--paper);
    stroke-width: 1.5;
  }

  .end {
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  figcaption {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-top: 0.3rem;
  }

  .key {
    display: inline-block;
    width: 1.4rem;
    height: 0;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--c);
    vertical-align: middle;
    margin-right: 0.3rem;
  }

  .key.dash {
    border-top-style: dashed;
    margin-left: 1rem;
  }

  .work {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-left: 1.2rem;
  }

  ol,
  ul {
    padding-left: 1.3rem;
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }

  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 0.8rem 0 1.2rem;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  th,
  td {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.8rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  thead th {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  tbody th {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .num {
    text-align: right;
  }

  .end-row th,
  .end-row td {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
